---
import ComparisonPage from "../components/ComparisonPage.astro";
import ComparisonSection from "../components/ComparisonSection.astro";
---

{/*
  Astro drops the whitespace of a line break that sits right next to an inline element, so the
  prose below only ever wraps between two plain words, never before or after <code> or <strong>.
*/}
<ComparisonPage
  competitor="shepherd"
  title="GlowTour.js vs Shepherd.js - Product Tour Library Comparison"
  description="Looking for a Shepherd.js alternative? Compare GlowTour.js and Shepherd.js across framework integrations, APIs, customization, accessibility and licensing."
  intro="Shepherd.js is a mature, framework-agnostic tour library; GlowTour.js is a newer one built around native framework adapters. If you are looking for a Shepherd.js alternative, the biggest differences are framework integration, UI composition, workflow APIs and licensing."
  glowtourBestFor="You want native framework adapters, a chainable workflow builder, component-level UI composition and an MIT license for commercial or open-source projects."
  competitorBestFor="You prefer a mature framework-agnostic library with official wrappers and your project is compatible with Shepherd.js licensing terms."
  faq={[
    {
      question: "Is GlowTour.js a Shepherd.js alternative?",
      answer:
        "Yes. Both create guided product tours across several frontend stacks. GlowTour.js focuses on native framework adapters, composable components and an MIT license; Shepherd.js offers a mature core with official wrappers.",
    },
    {
      question: "What is the licensing difference between GlowTour.js and Shepherd.js?",
      answer:
        "GlowTour.js is MIT licensed. Shepherd.js is offered under AGPL-3.0 with a commercial license available. Review the current project licenses before choosing one for a commercial application.",
    },
    {
      question: "Can I migrate a Shepherd.js tour to GlowTour.js?",
      answer:
        "The main concepts map directly: Shepherd steps become builder steps, attachTo targets become selectors, and tour controls map to the GlowTour instance. The APIs differ, so step definitions and callbacks need to be rewritten.",
    },
  ]}
>
  <ComparisonSection
    id="framework-integrations"
    heading="Framework integrations: official wrappers vs native adapters"
  >
    <p>
      Shepherd.js supports React, Vue and Angular through official wrappers maintained by the
      Shepherd team, which the project describes as wrappers around its core library. A community
      wrapper exists for Ember.
    </p>
    <p>
      GlowTour.js ships one adapter per framework that renders the tour with that framework's own
      components: React, Vue, Angular, Solid, and vanilla custom elements.
    </p>
  </ComparisonSection>

  <ComparisonSection
    id="customization-model"
    heading="Customization model: step options vs composable components"
  >
    <p>
      Shepherd steps are configured with options such as <code>title</code>, <code>text</code>, <code
        >buttons</code
      >, <code>classes</code> and <code>attachTo</code>, and render into
      Shepherd's dialog structure (header, text, footer). Its default styles are minimal and meant
      to be themed with your own CSS classes, per tour or per step.
    </p>
    <p>
      GlowTour.js exposes those dialog pieces as components you can reorder, replace or wrap, and
      themes them through <code>--glow-tour-*</code> CSS custom properties.
    </p>
  </ComparisonSection>

  <ComparisonSection id="api-ergonomics" heading="API ergonomics: addStep() vs a builder">
    <p>
      Shepherd builds a tour imperatively: create a <code>Tour</code>, call <code>addStep()</code> for
      each step, then control it with methods such as <code>start()</code>, <code>next()</code> and <code
        >back()</code
      >.
    </p>
    <p>
      GlowTour.js chains steps and step actions in a builder that returns an immutable workflow
      definition any adapter can run, and accepts a JSON config for tours stored outside your code.
    </p>
  </ComparisonSection>

  <ComparisonSection id="accessibility-licensing" heading="Accessibility and licensing">
    <p>
      Both document keyboard navigation, focus trapping and ARIA attributes. Licensing is the sharper
      difference: Shepherd.js is available under AGPL-3.0 for open source and non-commercial use,
      with a commercial license required for commercial products and revenue-generating companies.
      GlowTour.js is MIT licensed.
    </p>
  </ComparisonSection>

  <ComparisonSection id="which-to-choose" heading="Should you choose GlowTour.js or Shepherd.js?">
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Choose Shepherd.js</strong> if you want a mature framework-agnostic solution with
        official framework wrappers, and AGPL-3.0 or a commercial license fits your project.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Choose GlowTour.js</strong> if you want native framework adapters, a popover you
        compose from components, a builder-style API, and an MIT license.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </ComparisonSection>
</ComparisonPage>
